@meta
id: 18-04
title: Browse the tree with netrw
chapter: 18
type: lesson
skills: netrw
par: 62
minutes: 5
vscode: the Explorer sidebar
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts

@brief
Sometimes you do not know the file's name, only roughly where it is.
Vim ships with a file browser called netrw. Ask to edit a directory and
you get a listing of it instead of a file.

You are in the order provider. Somewhere under `src/infrastructure/`
there is a service that sends email, and its `post` call goes to
`/send`. Every other service on that gateway is namespaced, so the path
should be `/email/send`. Find the file by browsing, not by typing its
name, and make that one change.

Two ways in. `:e .` lists the project root. `:Explore` lists the
directory of the file you are in, which is usually closer to where you
want to be. The listing is an ordinary buffer: `j` and `k` move, `/`
searches, Enter opens the entry under the cursor. A directory entry
opens as another listing. `-` goes up one level.

The dojo keeps a couple of dot-files in the root. Leave them be.

@keys
| `:e .` | browse the current working directory |
| `:Explore` or `:Ex` | browse the directory of the current file |
| `Enter` | open the file or directory under the cursor |
| `-` | go up to the parent directory |
| `/name` | move to an entry, as in any buffer |

@hints
`:Ex` shows `src/domain/order/`. You need to go up twice and then down
into `infrastructure/services/`.
--
`-` goes up. Search for the directory you want and press Enter on it.
`/infra`, Enter, Enter is faster than counting lines, and it still works
when someone adds a directory above it.
--
`:Ex`, `--`, `/infra` Enter Enter, `/serv` Enter Enter, `/email`
Enter Enter. Then `/post`, `f/`, `aemail/`, `Esc`, `:wq`.

@solution
    :Ex<Enter> - -
    /infra<Enter><Enter> /serv<Enter><Enter> /email<Enter><Enter>
    /post<Enter> f/ aemail/ <Esc> :wq<Enter>

Or `:e .` and go down from the root: `/src`, `/infra`, `/serv`,
`/email`, Enter after each. About the same number of keys. Pick the
starting point that is nearer the target.

Notice that the listing is searchable. People who come from a sidebar
reach for `j` and start counting; `/` plus three letters is faster and
does not go wrong when the directory changes shape.

netrw is not a good sidebar and it is not trying to be. It is good at
exactly this: you are in a file, you want to look around it, you look,
you open something, the listing goes away. `:Sexplore` and `:Vexplore`
do the same in a split when you want the code to stay on screen. The
scenario at the end of the chapter uses one.

@start src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async cancel(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    await this.paymentService.refund(order.id);

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'CANCELLED' },
    });
  }
}

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
    };
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/email.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class EmailService {
  public async send(args: { to: string; template: string }) {
    return this.http.post(`/send`, args);
  }
}

@expected src/infrastructure/services/email.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class EmailService {
  public async send(args: { to: string; template: string }) {
    return this.http.post(`/email/send`, args);
  }
}

@start src/infrastructure/services/payment.service.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';

@injectable()
export class PaymentService {
  public async refund(orderId: string) {
    const response = await this.http.post(`/refunds`, { orderId });

    if (response.status !== 200) {
      throw exception('REFUND_FAILED');
    }

    return response.data;
  }
}
